---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import FactList from '../../components/FactList.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Services | Joshua Abrogena"
  description="Two engineering services, each tied to the project that demonstrates it, with its evidence and its limits stated."
>
  <div class="container wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Services</p>
    <h1>What I can do, and the proof behind each</h1>
    <p class="lede">These are capabilities I have demonstrated in two personal projects. I have no clients to list, no revenue and no production users, and I do not imply any. Each service names the project that shows it, labels its evidence, and says where it stops.</p>

    <nav class="jump" aria-label="Services">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#backend">Backend systems that stay correct</a></li>
        <li><a href="#aws">AWS infrastructure as code</a></li>
        <li><a href="#not">What I do not claim</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <Section n={1} id="backend" title="Backend systems that stay correct under retries and failure">
      <p>For systems where a duplicate, a crash or a race has a real cost: taking payments, holding inventory, running background jobs.</p>
      <h3>What the work looks like</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Payment webhook handlers that are safe to receive twice, late, or out of order, including the refund path when the money cannot be honoured.</li>
        <li>Stock reservation and order state machines where every legal and illegal move is tested.</li>
        <li>Outbox and job-queue workers with leases, retries, a sweeper and crash-recovery tests that kill the worker on purpose.</li>
        <li>Database design that does real work: constraints, locking order and transaction boundaries chosen deliberately.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>How I approach it</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The database is the source of truth, and the queue only carries a hint. That tolerates duplicates by design.</li>
        <li>Every status change goes through one function that checks one table, so no code path can skip the rules.</li>
        <li>Test what the code actually builds, not only what a helper accepts. RyuZCloud's CloudFront bug passed unit tests because they signed a hand-written string.</li>
        <li>Write the tradeoff down next to the decision, including what it costs.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Evidence</h3>
      <p>Shown in <a href="/projects/ryustorefront/">RyuStoreFront</a> and <a href="/projects/ryuzcloud/">RyuZCloud</a>. Each line is a short form; the setup, dataset and caveats are in the case studies.</p>
      <FactList compact ids={['ssfWebhookDupes', 'ssfStockRace', 'rzCrash', 'rzSeed']} />
      <h3>Where it stops</h3>
      <p>No load testing and no real traffic. Test results are my own records, and the correctness tests ran on my machine.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section n={2} id="aws" title="AWS infrastructure as code, deployed and torn down">
      <p>For getting a service onto AWS with Terraform in a way that can be reviewed, repeated and removed.</p>
      <h3>What the work looks like</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Terraform for ECS Fargate services, RDS PostgreSQL, load balancing with certificates, S3 with CloudFront and origin access control, SQS with a dead-letter queue, Secrets Manager and least-privilege IAM.</li>
        <li>A persistent foundation stage (DNS zone, registry, certificate, secret shells) separate from a destroyable per-session stage, so a teardown cannot touch DNS.</li>
        <li>An audit script that checks each service directly for leftovers after teardown, because tag searches lag behind deletions.</li>
        <li>Working inside short-lived credentials: staged applies, and recovery from an apply or destroy cut off by expired tokens.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>How I approach it</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Save the plan to a file and read it before every apply. Check the numbers: resources to add, change and destroy.</li>
        <li>Say what a cost saving costs. Public-subnet tasks without a NAT gateway are cheaper and less isolated, so I do not call that a private network.</li>
        <li>Deploy early, because the surprises are cloud-only. Record every result as run, user-reported or not run.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Evidence</h3>
      <p>Shown in <a href="/projects/ryustorefront/">RyuStoreFront</a> and <a href="/projects/ryuzcloud/">RyuZCloud</a>. Each line is a short form; the setup, dataset and caveats are in the case studies.</p>
      <FactList compact ids={['ssfCloudCheckout', 'ssfCloudChecks', 'rzPrivate', 'rzCloud']} />
      <h3>Where it stops</h3>
      <p>Both projects are live now, but every AWS number was measured in earlier sessions, not on the current deployments. There is no measured cost and no load test. In both projects every apply ran from a saved plan that I read first.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section id="not" title="What I do not claim">
      <ul>
        <li>No clients, no paid work, no revenue and no production users.</li>
        <li>No scale, throughput, latency or uptime figures. None of the projects has a load test.</li>
        <li>No distributed-systems expertise. The evidence shows single-database backends and small AWS deployments.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Full-stack work (Next.js against a real API, Playwright and axe checks) appears in both projects. I list it as a capability rather than a service.</p>
      <p>How I check and label results is on the <a href="/about/#how-i-work">About page</a>.</p>
      <p><a href="/contact/">Contact</a> &middot; <a href="/projects/">All projects</a></p>
    </Section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .wrap { padding-block: var(--space-8) 0; }
  h1 { font-size: var(--step-4); margin-top: var(--space-3); max-width: 20ch; }
  .lede { color: var(--color-text-muted); max-width: var(--measure); margin: var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-6); }
  .jump ol { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-6); padding: 0; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-5); }
  .jump a { display: inline-block; padding: var(--space-2) 0; min-height: 44px; box-sizing: border-box; }
</style>
